---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fillDurations } from '../lib/time';
import pkg from '../../package.json';
import { CAST } from '../lib/critters/sprites';

const deps = Object.keys(pkg.dependencies);
const frames = Object.values(CAST).reduce((n, c) => n + Object.keys(c.frames).length, 0);
const meta = fillDurations('{meta}');
const source = 'https://github.com/njhazelh/nick-jones.me';
---

<Base title="Colophon" description="How this site is built, the budgets it holds and the options I turned down.">
  <h1>How this site is built</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This page is the design doc for the site: what it's made of, the limits it holds itself to, and the options I
    turned down. The <a href={source}>source is public</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Materials</h2>
  <p>
    Plain HTML and CSS, generated by <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> from Markdown files in the repository.
    Every page reads the same with JavaScript turned off. One small script draws the critters, and the
    readout at the foot of each page gives its size. The site has {deps.length} direct dependencies: {deps.join(', ')}.
  </p>
  <p>
    The type is Source Serif 4, served from this domain in three files: regular, italic and semibold. A page downloads
    only the ones it uses. Labels, dates and figures are set in your system's own monospace font, so they cost nothing to
    download. The colours are warm plaster, slate and one brick red. The dark theme uses the same materials with the
    lights off. Every type, colour and spacing decision sits in one file, so a redesign touches that file and no content.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chart on the home page, repeated small on every preview card, is an illustration, not data: frame times that run
    over a 60 Hz budget until a fix, then stay under it. It's an SVG drawn when the site is built, from a seeded generator, so every build draws the same one.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a page is shared, the preview card is an image drawn at build time from the page's own title, in the same type
    and colours. It's made once per page, not by a service on each share.
  </p>

  <h2>The critters</h2>
  <p>
    A tiger cub, jellyfish, a ladybird and a critter for the time of year live around the text. They're there to be
    played with. Hover over one to see what it's doing, click it, or pick it up and throw it. The tiger chases your
    pointer. The ladybird is the one bug I leave in on purpose, and it hides: it could be anywhere on the page, and it
    moves if you lunge at it. The tiger hasn't caught it yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fourth critter changes with the calendar. A ghost haunts October, a turkey poult turns up for Thanksgiving
    week, a reindeer calf for the winter holidays, a lovebird the week before Valentine's Day, a frog for St Patrick's
    Day and a bunny the week before Easter. The rest of the year it's the season's: a penguin chick in winter, a
    butterfly in spring, a firefly in summer and a hedgehog in autumn.
  </p>
  <p>
    They're drawn a pixel at a time, {frames} frames in all, and scaled up by a whole number of screen pixels so they
    stay sharp. The jellyfish and the season's critter keep to the margins. The tiger starts at the top of the page and
    climbs the text. It stands on the tops of lines and headings wherever there is room above them, jumps from one to
    the next, and climbs the side of a paragraph to reach the line above. When you scroll away, it follows you.
  </p>
  <p>
    They're all drawn under the page, so the words are always on top. The tiger and the ladybird only stop where they
    cover none of them, and a check on every pull request looks for a word under a resting critter on every page. On a
    phone, where the text fills the screen, the others wait on the line above the footer while the tiger roams the page,
    climbing the edges of the screen. If you move through a page with the keyboard, a jellyfish keeps level with
    whatever has focus.
  </p>
  <p>
    The script that moves them was written for them, with no library. It starts after the page has loaded, stops in a
    background tab, and lets them fall asleep when nobody has been near them for a minute. If your system asks for
    reduced motion, they keep still. They never print. The Critters switch at the foot of every page turns them off,
    and the site remembers.
  </p>

  <h2>Budgets</h2>
  <p>
    I find what is slow or broken in large systems for a living, so a slow personal site would argue against me. Every
    deploy runs these checks, and a failure stops the deploy.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Limit</th><th scope="col">How it's checked</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Home page under 100 KB transferred</td><td>Measured from the built files after every build</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Under 24 KB of JavaScript on any page, and none needed to read it</td><td>Measured from the built files after every build, and every page is checked with JavaScript off on every pull request</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No request to any other host</td><td>The built HTML, CSS and JavaScript are scanned for outside addresses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No phone number anywhere</td><td>The built pages are scanned for anything shaped like one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No copy that goes stale</td><td>A lint fails the build on words that pin a sentence to a moment or promise a schedule</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lighthouse 95 or higher in every category</td><td>Run on every pull request</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WCAG 2.1 AA, light and dark</td><td>axe runs on every page in both themes on every pull request</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The readout at the foot of each page reports that page's weight, its request count, how much of it is JavaScript,
    and the build time. It is written after the build from the files themselves, so it can't drift from the truth.
  </p>

  <h2>Copy that doesn't go stale</h2>
  <p>
    Anything that dates, such as my role, employer and start dates, lives in one data file. Durations like "{meta}" are computed when the site is built, so they move on their own.
  </p>
  <p>
    A post or project is a Markdown file. It stays a draft until I say otherwise, and pushing it publishes it. A post
    can name the project it's about, and the project's page lists it, so the writing and the work stay connected.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing on the site shows how long it has been since the last post. The home page links to pieces I chose, not the
    newest ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Turned down</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Chose</th><th scope="col">Over</th><th scope="col">Because</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Static HTML</td>
        <td>A JavaScript framework in the browser</td>
        <td>Reading a page shouldn't need a program to run first</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Canvases under the page</td>
        <td>A canvas over the page</td>
        <td>Nothing should sit between you and a sentence, even a transparent layer</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Critters drawn by hand</td>
        <td>A sprite or game library</td>
        <td>The cast and the script that moves them weigh less than one of the fonts</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Fonts served from this domain</td>
        <td>A font CDN</td>
        <td>A font CDN is a request to someone else's server, and it tells them who is reading</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Markdown in Git</td>
        <td>A CMS</td>
        <td>Every change is a diff, and the content moves to another generator in a weekend</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>No analytics</td>
        <td>A tracking script</td>
        <td>I don't need the numbers enough to make you carry the script</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Replies by email</td>
        <td>Comments</td>
        <td>Comments need moderation, and moderation is upkeep</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>DigitalOcean App Platform</td>
        <td>GitHub Pages</td>
        <td>My other small apps already deploy there, so there is one way to ship</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Whitespace and a grid</td>
        <td>Banners, icons and boxes</td>
        <td>Every visible thing should do a job</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What I'd change</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The host tells browsers to keep files for only 10 seconds, fonts included. Each repeat visit re-checks them. The
      answer is a cheap "not modified", but it's still a round trip I'd remove with a caching rule in front of the host.
    </li>
    <li>
      The resume has no PDF download yet. Printing one needs a browser, and the build machine doesn't have one. Your
      browser's print command gives a clean copy in the meantime.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  table {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-variant-numeric: lining-nums;
  }
  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.45rem 0.75rem 0.45rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  th {
    border-bottom-color: var(--rule-strong);
  }
  td:first-child {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
